From b3b3d5730ed2cc06e0921788b1da163054cecca8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 17:38:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][3D] A 3D video that stops brings the menus back
 whole, also at the resolution it played in

After a 3D video of 48 frames a second (MV-HEVC) on a 3840x2160 desktop
the menus stayed side by side, the left eye alone: "the interface broke
and stayed in SBS but only showed the left eye".

The video played at the menus' own resolution, 3840x2160 at 60 Hz (the
display has no 48 Hz mode). When it stopped, the change of resolution
that turns 3D off with it (one mode set where there were two) set the
stereo mode to off and asked for the menus' resolution without forcing
it, and the graphic context skips a change to the resolution it is in:
the sizes of the view stayed those of an eye, half the width, and the
menus were drawn into the left half of the screen. Where the video had
another rate (a 24p film) the change went through and nothing showed.

The change is now forced, as the change of the stereo mode in Flip()
always was: the sizes of the whole screen, one mode set without a 3D
output, and kodi.log says it as before.
---
 xbmc/windowing/GraphicContext.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/GraphicContext.cpp b/xbmc/windowing/GraphicContext.cpp
index 8c28604..d92f1c2 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/GraphicContext.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/GraphicContext.cpp
@@ -383,7 +383,12 @@ void CGraphicContext::SetFullScreenVideo(bool bOnOff)
                            "3D off, in one change of mode");
         m_stereoMode = RenderStereoMode::OFF;
         m_nextStereoMode = RenderStereoMode::OFF;
-        SetVideoResolution(targetResolutionOnStop, false);
+        // (forced, as a change of the stereo mode is in Flip(): the sizes of the
+        // view are those of an eye until the resolution is set again, and the menus' resolution is
+        // often the one the video played in, 2160p60 for a film of 48 frames a second, which a
+        // change that is not forced leaves as it is: the menus were drawn into the left half,
+        // stretched by a display still in 3D, the left eye alone)
+        SetVideoResolution(targetResolutionOnStop, true);
         CServiceBroker::GetGUI()->GetWindowManager().SendMessage(GUI_MSG_NOTIFY_ALL, 0, 0,
                                                                  GUI_MSG_RENDERER_RESET);
       }
